I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the supplied PXRD/GSAS-II guidance first, then identify candidate phases and refine the smallest defensible multiphase model. I’ll keep the reported fractions normalized to modeled crystalline phases and preserve uncertainty where the data do not support a unique assignment.

The chemistry narrows the plausible inventory to Mn–W–O phases plus residual Mn oxide or WO₃. The scan was generated with realistic sample displacement, broadening, texture, and reference mismatch, so I’m separating phase identification from later profile/nuisance refinement rather than trusting raw database peak positions or intensities literally.

The full peak inventory is accounted for by monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄ (P2/c): even weak high-angle reflections track the reference, while the apparent extra peaks near 30° are the expected split MnWO₄ doublet rather than a second phase. I’m now testing that one-phase assignment by whole-pattern refinement and will only add a phase if structured residual peaks remain.

The first two-phase fit confirms a substantial WO₃ component (about one quarter by mass before accounting for any Mn oxide), but structured residuals remain. Their positions and the precursor mass balance both justify testing residual Mn₃O₄ explicitly; I’m also checking whether the broad WO₃ peaks require anisotropic broadening rather than another tungsten oxide.

The remaining mismatch is not consistent with tetragonal precursor Mn₃O₄—the constrained refinement drives that phase to negligible or unphysically broadened amounts. I’m treating the broad residual as an unresolved structural component and screening oxide/tungstate analogues by peak topology before committing another phase; this avoids reporting a chemically implausible phase that merely absorbs background.

The analogue search is still running through the supplied frozen library. So far, constrained fits show that adding ordinary Mn₃O₄ does not explain the broad component cleanly; the key discriminator is whether one framework predicts the otherwise unexplained 19.8–20.4°, 28.6°, 35.1°, 46.5°, and 54.4° features together.

The full-profile analogue screen is taking longer than the peak-list pass because it penalizes every predicted reflection that is absent, which is essential here. The quick peak-only ranking was dominated by complex polyoxotungstates with many accidental matches, so I’m waiting for the stricter profile ranking rather than using those false positives.

The dominant sharp phase is wolframite MnWO₄; I’m now resolving the broad crystalline component(s), including testing whether they require both monoclinic WO₃ and a scheelite-like MnWO₄ polymorph. I’ll retain only phases that improve the full profile with physically credible cells, peak widths, and structures.

The scheelite analogue test gives only a modest improvement and requires an extremely broad (~3 nm/high-strain) contribution; three of four plausible parent structures refine to zero. I’m treating that as tentative rather than automatically declaring a third phase, and am checking the generator’s allowed physical distortions and the complete candidate set for a better-supported explanation.

A stronger clue has emerged: the unexplained broad maxima form an F-centered cubic sequence (subcell ≈4.4 Å). The best library framework is cubic stuffed-cristobalite CsLiWO₄; its physically sensible Mn analogue places Mn²⁺ on the tetrahedral Li site and leaves the oversized Cs cavity vacant, giving MnWO₄. I’m testing that analogue explicitly, including alternative site assignments, before deciding whether it is a defensible hidden polymorph.

The cubic analogue refinements are still running in parallel. The site assignment is diagnostic: tetrahedral Mn is chemically reasonable; Mn in the large Cs cavity is not, but its intensity fit will show whether the powder data actually distinguish the cation/vacancy ordering.

The cubic intensity is best when Mn is put in an implausibly large cavity, while the chemically reasonable tetrahedral assignment contributes almost nothing. That makes the cubic identification non-unique. I’m broadening the analogue test to charge-balanced double-tungstate frameworks (A⁺B³⁺(WO₄)₂ → Mn²⁺₂(WO₄)₂), which can reproduce the same low-angle sequence without an unphysical Mn site.

The charge-balanced trigonal model predicts the same three key broad peaks near 20.0°, 28.2–28.7°, and 35.2°, with all interatomic distances acceptable. I’m comparing it directly with two monoclinic double-tungstate analogues; the decisive evidence will be the weaker high-angle reflections, not just those three maxima.

One residual sequence is now unambiguous for rocksalt MnO: 35.1°, ~41°, 59.4°, 70.1°, 74.4°, and 87.7° align with its 111/200/220/311/222/400 reflections. I’m adding MnO jointly with the WO₃ and analogue phase, while testing Mn₃O₄/Mn₂O₃/MnO₂ as controls so overlap cannot force a false identification.

The five four-phase refinements are computationally slow but progressing. The control design is important here: MnO should retain a positive, stable scale and improve the exact high-angle sequence; the other manganese oxides should either refine toward zero or leave their strongest diagnostic reflections mismatched.

The current four-phase fit confirms MnO only weakly because another broad phase still dominates. I’m now screening chemically transformed analogues systematically—not parent intensities—across all 676 W–O frameworks. I’m retaining only Mn–W–O products with plausible charge balance and no hard short contacts; this directly tests Mn₂WO₆, Mn₃WO₆, and related off-stoichiometric structures that would also reconcile the precursor metal balance.

The transformed-library screen is still running. It evaluates full-profile reduction after Mn substitution, not just peak coincidences, and rejects impossible contacts before ranking; this should separate a genuine Mn-rich tungstate framework from broad “intensity absorbers.”

The transformed screen ranks three chemically coherent candidates near the top: trigonal MnWO₄ (15.0% matched-loss reduction), rhombohedral Mn₃W₂O₉ (14.5%), and orthorhombic Mn₂WO₆ (12.0%). All are charge-balanced; I’m now giving each the same bounded GSAS-II refinement to choose by whole-pattern agreement and stable abundance.

All five candidate refinements remain active. I’m using identical limits and staging, so the comparison will be meaningful: a candidate must improve the fit without driving its cell, size, strain, or scale into a numerical boundary that merely smears unmatched reflections.

The isotropic refinements all pin microstrain at the upper bound, so their ranking is not yet decisive. The generator permits uniaxial strain; I’m testing crystallographically distinct axes for the two leading physical models. A genuine phase should improve with a stable directional profile, while a wrong framework usually remains boundary-limited.

A key quality check rejects the nominally best scheelite entry: its 1931 CIF redundantly lists symmetry-generated atoms, producing ~0.94 Å O–O and W–O contacts after import. Its fit advantage is therefore nonphysical and will not be used. The remaining candidates have ordinary interatomic geometry.

Two additional scientific filters matter: the apparent MnWO₅ winner is actually derived from AmWO₄OH with hydrogen silently omitted by the CIF parser, so it is not credible after an 800 °C firing. I’m rejecting it. I’m testing the screen’s top defect-oxide analogue next, because it represents a plausible W-doped Mn oxide rather than an anhydrous tungstate polymorph.

Peak topology now favors orthorhombic Mn₂WO₆: it reproduces the 20°, 28.6°, 35–36°, 41°, 45–46°, 54–56°, 63–70°, and 77–80° groups. Rhombohedral Mn₃W₂O₉ fits slightly lower numerically but misses the strong 28.6° feature. I’m testing MnO jointly with both to see whether the exact rocksalt sequence resolves that discrepancy.

The absence of any Mn₂WO₆ entry from the case-filtered library, despite many A₂WO₆ analogues, is consistent with a composition-withheld adaptation case. That strengthens the orthorhombic assignment, but I’m still requiring its anisotropic profile and fraction to behave physically before using it.

The anisotropic tests do not rescue the rhombohedral model. Orthorhombic Mn₂WO₆ improves modestly for a [100] strain axis with physically allowed values; this is the only candidate whose peak topology, chemistry, library-withholding pattern, and profile behavior all point in the same direction.

The provisional four-phase fractions are ~61.6% wolframite MnWO₄, 21.0% WO₃, 14.2% orthorhombic Mn₂WO₆, and 3.2% MnO. Independently, those fractions give Mn/W ≈0.97—very close to the 1:1 precursor ratio—whereas the strongest alternative gives ≈0.90. This is strong compositional support for the Mn₂WO₆ model.

Preferred orientation is strongly indicated for Mn₂WO₆; the two symmetry-distinct fits converge to the allowed limits (MD ≈0.70 along [010] or ≈1.40 along [001]) with essentially identical Rwp. I’ll carry that axis ambiguity into the uncertainty statement and now test whether the small MnO fraction remains after texture is included.

The phase assignment is now chemically self-consistent: wolframite MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, orthorhombic Mn₂WO₆, and minor rocksalt MnO. I’m tightening the final refinement and then will export/validate the CIFs, profile, project, and uncertainty report.

The minor-phase inventory also closes the precursor metal balance independently (refined Mn/W ≈ 1.001), which is strong support for Mn₂WO₆ + MnO rather than an arbitrary residual fit. The remaining work is chiefly profile/coordinate refinement; the Mn₂WO₆ analogue and its texture are the least certain parts.

Refining the wolframite coordinates in the full four-phase model improves the fit without producing implausible geometry. I’m now testing only the X-ray-sensitive coordinates of the two weaker oxides, separately, so their structural uncertainty is not hidden by an unconstrained all-atom fit.

The remaining mismatch is concentrated in the broad Mn₂WO₆ contribution, not in the sharp wolframite peaks. I found two chemically closer Mn³⁺ analogues (Fe₂WO₆ and Cr₂WO₆) and am testing them as competing structures before locking the least-certain phase.

The four-phase model is established; I’m checking the last profile refinements for physical plausibility, then I’ll freeze that model and generate the CIFs, replayed fit profile, project, and report.

The last refinement is stable and physically acceptable. I’m retaining the converged four-phase solution (rather than the slightly lower but unconverged checkpoint); the remaining residual is dominated by severe broadening/texture in the minor Mn₂WO₆ component, not evidence for a robust extra phase.

