I found one worthwhile robustness check before freezing the scientific interpretation: the analogue-derived Mn₂WO₆ lattice fits, but unconstrained atom refinement had previously improved the profile by creating impossible short bonds. I’m testing a restrained coordinate refinement that enforces plausible W–O and Mn–O coordination; I’ll keep it only if both the replayed fit and geometry improve.

The restrained-coordinate trial was rejected: although it lowered the numerical residual, it violated its own bond restraints and produced multiple sub-ångström/overlong contacts. The submitted model therefore keeps the physically valid analogue coordinates and treats Mn₂WO₆’s exact atom positions as the main structural uncertainty. I’m now writing the replayed profile and normalized quantitative result.

The independent checks now pass: all four CIFs match the phase models replayed from `final.gpx`, all structures pass the contact/occupancy screen, the fractions sum exactly to one, and `fit.csv` reproduces the 8301-point observed grid with the reported Rwp.

